1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Character lines. Rossie is a woman—let’s turn her into a man.

2
00:01:21,714 --> 00:01:31,714
The model is starting to load into VRAM; this might take a few minutes on the first run. Keep an eye on GPU usage.

3
00:02:43,429 --> 00:02:53,429
In the meantime, let’s process the character lines. The first 16 lines are already underway—that’s the first batch.

4
00:04:05,143 --> 00:04:15,143
I had to reduce the batch size because the VRAM was overflowing. If you set the batch size too high,

5
00:05:26,857 --> 00:05:36,857
the GPU can’t keep up and starts using the PC’s system RAM, which you’ll definitely notice—the system will start swapping.

6
00:06:48,571 --> 00:06:58,571
That’s better now. The batch size was too big. We’re at 45—halfway there.

7
00:08:10,286 --> 00:08:20,286
Finding the right balance for your specific hardware takes a bit of time; it’s not just a "press a button and forget about it"

8
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:42,000
kind of thing. We’re at 81 out of 112, with about 51 seconds left. Done! 128 lines in about 8 minutes—including my own testing.

9
00:10:53,714 --> 00:11:03,714
It doesn’t sound drunk; let’s change that—we’ll switch to mode C.

10
00:12:15,429 --> 00:12:25,429
If you change the voice, you have to overwrite the previously generated .wav files.

11
00:13:37,143 --> 00:13:47,143
The video is already 26 minutes of pure boredom, but hey, you asked for a tutorial, so here it is! :D We’re halfway through.

12
00:14:58,857 --> 00:15:08,857
At least you get to see how to troubleshoot issues—63 out of 128 done. It’s all about getting the settings right.

13
00:16:20,571 --> 00:16:30,571
On a CPU, 128 lines would probably take an hour or so. We’re almost finished; I’ll play them back in a moment.

14
00:17:42,286 --> 00:17:52,286
Let me listen to a few. It should sound fine now.

15
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:14,000
I mean, if that didn’t sound like a drunk person, I honestly don’t know what does! :D Let me play it again.

16
00:20:25,714 --> 00:20:35,714
If you want it to sound even more like a drunk person, you’ll need to write it that way in the dialogue itself.

17
00:21:47,429 --> 00:21:57,429
If you write it the way the AI wrote it in the prompt, that’s exactly how it’s going to sound. "Ah, hold on...

18
00:23:09,143 --> 00:23:19,143
hold on a second, lads, listen to this—you're never gonna believe it! I was just...

19
00:24:30,857 --> 00:24:40,857
just sitting there in the Astral Lounge, right? Minding me own business, having a quiet little sip of Bog's Grog...

20
00:25:52,571 --> 00:26:02,571
when this, this absolute gobshite from New Atlantis knocks me Chunks right off the table! Straight onto the floor! Well, I...

21
00:27:14,286 --> 00:27:24,286
I completely lost the head, didn't I?

22
00:28:35,1000 --> 00:28:45,1000
Threw a wild right hook, smashed three chairs, and next thing I know—hic—half of Neon security is chasing me down the docks!

23
00:29:57,714 --> 00:30:07,714
Still got me drink though... ha! Best night in the Settled Systems, I swear to God!" Get it? Alright, jokes aside.

24
00:31:19,429 --> 00:31:29,429
Hopefully, that helped you out. It was a bit of a bore,

25
00:32:41,143 --> 00:32:51,143
but I can show you how to create .wem files and generate facial expressions using FaceGen via the Creation Kit (CK).

26
00:34:02,857 --> 00:34:12,857
It’s lip-syncing—meaning the character will react and articulate according to the text. So,

27
00:35:24,571 --> 00:35:34,571
if you write it correctly—for instance,

28
00:36:46,286 --> 00:36:56,286
specifying that the character is very angry—they’ll have both the voice and the facial expression to match. That’s a wrap!

